---
import Docs from "~/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import PhantomBadge from "~/components/PhantomBadge.astro";
---

<Docs
  title="Phantom Secrets"
  description="Stack is the control plane; Phantom is the vault. Understanding the split is the single most important mental model for operating Stack safely."
  eyebrow="Integrations"
>
  <div class="not-prose mb-8 flex items-center gap-4 p-4 panel rounded-xl" style="border-left: 2px solid #3b82f6;">
    <img
      src="/brand/phantom/logo.svg"
      alt=""
      width="48"
      height="48"
      style="flex-shrink: 0"
      aria-hidden="true"
    />
    <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-[#3b82f6] mb-1">
        Sister product
      </div>
      <div class="text-[15px] text-[color:var(--color-ink-100)] leading-snug">
        <strong class="font-semibold">Phantom Secrets</strong> — E2E-encrypted vault.
        Credential storage and execution require supported setup.
      </div>
    </div>
    <a
      href="https://phm.dev"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
      class="mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.08em] px-3 py-2 border border-[#3b82f6]/40 text-[color:var(--color-ink-100)] hover:bg-[#3b82f6]/10 hover:border-[#3b82f6] hover:text-[#3b82f6] transition-colors rounded-md flex-shrink-0"
    >
      phm.dev ↗
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>The split</h2>
  <p>
    Setup handles supplied and materialized credential values in memory and
    attempts persistence through installed Phantom. Dedicated secrets in
    <code>.stack.toml</code> record the <em>slot name</em>; manual fields and
    freeform metadata can contain user data and must be reviewed before sharing.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-8 panel rounded-2xl p-6">
    <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="min-w-0">
        <div class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-magenta-400 mb-2">
          Stack (control plane)
        </div>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-[13px] text-ink-300">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-magenta-400 mt-0.5">●</span>
            <span class="min-w-0">Drives provider OAuth / API flows</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-magenta-400 mt-0.5">●</span>
            <span class="min-w-0">Creates or attaches resources according to the adapter</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-magenta-400 mt-0.5">●</span>
            <span class="min-w-0">Tracks which secrets each service needs (<em>slot names</em>)</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-magenta-400 mt-0.5">●</span>
            <span class="min-w-0">Exports empty-key <code class="mono text-[12px]">.env.example</code> skeletons separately; writes available <code class="mono text-[12px]">.mcp.json</code> descriptors</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-ink-500">
            <span class="mt-0.5">○</span>
            <span class="min-w-0">Handles credential values during setup</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="min-w-0">
        <div class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-cyan-300 mb-2">
          Phantom (vault)
        </div>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-[13px] text-ink-300">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-cyan-300 mt-0.5">●</span>
            <span class="min-w-0">Provides the installed credential-persistence interface</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-cyan-300 mt-0.5">●</span>
            <span class="min-w-0">Owns supported execution; <code class="mono text-[12px]">phm_*</code> strings are not generally resolved by Stack</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-cyan-300 mt-0.5">●</span>
            <span class="min-w-0">Requires separately qualified sync and CI configuration</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-cyan-300 mt-0.5">●</span>
            <span class="min-w-0">Controls credential access through its installed interface</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-ink-500">
            <span class="mt-0.5">○</span>
            <span class="min-w-0">Has no opinion on what the keys mean</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Why Stack refuses to run without Phantom</h2>
  <p>
    When you run <code>stack add</code>, <code>stack import</code>, or{" "}
    <code>stack doctor</code>, Stack calls <code>phantom</code> via its CLI. If
    Phantom setup is missing or its project metadata is unavailable, the
    credential-writing path refuses before provider contact. Illustrative refusal:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`$ stack add supabase
Phantom Secrets isn't installed.`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    Blank <code>stack init</code> can write split config without provider setup.
    Selected template init attempts setup by default; use <code>--noProvision</code>
    for config only. Other read-only commands have their own requirements.
  </p>

  <h2>The flow of a single secret</h2>
  <p>Take <code>SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY</code> as an example:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      You run <code>stack add supabase</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Where the OAuth branch is configured, Stack opens Supabase authorization and listens on
      <code>http://127.0.0.1:8787/callback</code> — register that exact URI as the
      allowed redirect in your OAuth app so no wildcard matching is needed.
    </li>
    <li>
      The adapter can create a project through the Management API or attach an
      existing resource; access and outcomes must be checked (example ref:
      <code>abcdefghijklmnop</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      The adapter attempts to fetch the <code>service_role</code> key. Setup
      processes credential values in memory.
    </li>
    <li>
      Stack submits <code>SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY</code> through the supported
      create-only Phantom stdin interface. Existing names, missing setup or
      refused persistence can stop the operation.
    </li>
    <li>
      Stack writes <code>secrets = ["SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY"]</code> into
      <code>.stack.toml</code>. <em>Earlier credential and MCP writes can remain if later writes fail.</em>
    </li>
    <li>
      Run <code>stack env export --example</code> separately for an empty
      <code>SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=</code> skeleton; Stack does not regenerate populated env files.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Check <code>phm_</code> strings before sharing.</strong> Stack does
    not establish their safety or general substitution behavior.
    <code>phantom exec</code> is an installed-product interface that
    <code>stack exec</code> forwards to; follow its supported configuration
    and verify the actual consumer.
  </div>

  <h2>Runtime injection — <code>stack exec</code></h2>
  <p>
    Configure the application inputs required by <code>process.env</code>
    using the installed Phantom guidance. The Stack wrapper alone does not
    establish that they are supplied:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`# Ordinary app startup; configure required inputs first
bun dev

# Forward through installed Phantom exec; verify the app receives its inputs
stack exec -- bun dev`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    <code>stack exec</code> forwards everything after <code>--</code> to
    <code>phantom exec</code>. Use it for dev servers, test runners, migrations —
    only after the app and installed execution path are configured and verified.
  </p>

  <h2>Syncing the vault</h2>
  <p>
    Sync and CI bootstrap belong to the installed Phantom product. Check its
    current capabilities and configuration before transferring any credentials:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`# Inspect the installed interface before planning sync
phantom --version
phantom --help`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    <code>stack ci init</code> (see <a href="/docs/ci/">CI integration</a>)
    now refuses workflow generation with exit 1 before creating a workflow directory or writing a file. Its config and existing-file guards remain; <code>stack ci init --force</code> also refuses without overwriting. Phantom Secrets 0.7.9 requires a trusted terminal for Cloud pull; <code>--force</code> does not bypass it, and <code>PHANTOM_CLOUD_TOKEN</code> is not a supported bearer-token input. The local vault passphrase is separate from the Cloud encryption key. No unattended Cloud bootstrap is provided.
  </p>

  <h2>Which Stack commands talk to Phantom</h2>

  <table tabindex="0" aria-label="Stack commands and Phantom usage">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 28%">Command</th>
        <th style="width: 20%">Needs Phantom?</th>
        <th>What it does with the vault</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stack init</code></td>
        <td>Config-only: no; service setup: required</td>
        <td>Blank/config-only init writes split config; selected templates attempt setup.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stack add</code></td>
        <td>Yes</td>
        <td>Writes provisioned secrets into the vault.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stack import</code></td>
        <td>Yes</td>
        <td>Attempts nonempty parsed values; existing vault names are skipped and writes can fail.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stack remove</code></td>
        <td>Yes</td>
        <td>Attempts named-secret cleanup; headless or refused cleanup can keep keys.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stack env show / diff</code></td>
        <td>Yes</td>
        <td>Reads the vault's key list (never the values).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stack doctor</code></td>
        <td>Yes</td>
        <td>Runs available adapter checks; some are absent or limited.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stack exec</code></td>
        <td>Yes</td>
        <td>Forwards to <code>phantom exec</code> — verify installed execution and the app separately.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stack list</code>, <code>stack info</code></td>
        <td>Command-dependent requirements</td>
        <td>List reads config; info checks slot presence and may run an adapter health check.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stack providers</code>, <code>stack templates list</code>, <code>stack projects list</code></td>
        <td>No</td>
        <td>Static catalog / registry data.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Installing Phantom</h2>
  <p>
    Follow the <a href="https://phm.dev/secrets">Phantom Secrets setup guidance</a>
    for your platform and verify its credential and execution interfaces.
    Stack source and this example do not establish an installed version or
    completed vault setup.
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`# Inspect the installed product before configuring Stack
phantom --version
phantom --help`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    For the full Phantom story, see <PhantomBadge size="md" showUrl={true} />.
  </p>
</Docs>
